WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.427 --> 00:00:02.844
(soft music)

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.080
- Here on the snowy slopes
of Mt. Hood, Oregon,

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:10.150
it seems impossible

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:12.430
that the U.S. could ever run low on water.

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:14.650
But government-backed
research says it could,

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:17.061
in little more than 50 years.

00:00:17.061 --> 00:00:20.210
(gurgling water)

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:23.220
Oregon relies heavily on snow
melt for its fresh water.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:24.800
But when the winter snows fail,

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.630
it can cause severe water
shortages in the summer.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:29.685
And those snows are failing more.

00:00:29.685 --> 00:00:32.720
(gentle music)

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:34.350
Today I've come to the City of Portland

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:36.163
to meet Kyle Anderson, lead architect

00:00:36.163 --> 00:00:39.480
of Hassalo on Eighth, an
innovative housing development

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.430
where technology is
helping to significantly

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:43.163
reduce residents' water footprint.

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:46.590
- The one thing about this project,

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:47.840
what makes it real unique,

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.720
is that we had the opportunity
to have critical mass.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.730
You know, to take three acres of asphalt

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:55.970
and bring on a million square feet

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:59.290
of 657 apartments, all
of a sudden the demand

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:01.780
for the resources becomes much higher.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:04.373
So, the savings become
that much higher as well.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:07.730
- And so talk to me about the red tanks.

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:09.340
This is the NORM system.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:10.920
- So, NORM is an acronym for

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.950
Natural Organic Recycling Machine.

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:15.670
This development treats
all black and gray water

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:17.360
on site for the residents.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:19.400
We basically are harvesting water.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.330
All of the apartments
for flushing toilets,

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:25.400
for watering gardens, we're
treating 45,000 gallons

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.313
of black and gray water daily.

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:30.840
And then we just celebrate it.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.630
It becomes part of the
vernacular of the architecture.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:35.900
We call it science on display.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:39.260
Actually drove people to this development.

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:40.700
As we build more and more

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.240
and we get denser and denser

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:43.860
this is going to become
more and more important.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.360
Maybe we should be
approaching infrastructure

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:46.520
in a different way.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:48.880
- There are a lot of simple
and accessible technologies

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:50.550
that have been put into this building

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:52.563
that we can use in all our homes.

00:01:54.024 --> 00:01:55.480
- Hi, good to see you.
- Hi, how are you?

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:57.700
- Talk to me a little bit
about the water saving

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:58.950
features in the building.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.893
- We have compact four
gallon dish washing system,

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:06.680
the low-flow shower, and
the front loading washers

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:07.660
and dryers.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:09.540
- You didn't think about
all these sustainable things

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:10.990
but they're already just built in for you.

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:12.950
- Sustainability by design, right?

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:15.660
And it's becoming like the new norm.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:17.610
- But many homes in the U.S. still use

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:19.440
standard top-loading washing machines

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.440
that can use around 45
gallons of water per load.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.780
But front-loaders use as little as 13.

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:27.010
- And many of us still
rinse our dirty dishes

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:28.900
under a running faucet before loading them

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:30.670
into the dishwasher.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.570
This is a huge waste of water

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:35.140
because you actually need
the dishes to be dirty

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:38.330
as the enzymes in the detergent
latch onto food particles

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:40.200
to work effectively.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.540
It's these small actions
done by housebuilders

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:44.350
and homeowners across America that can

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:46.110
add up to big water savings.

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:47.920
And that can make a huge difference

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:49.968
to our nation's water security.

00:02:49.968 --> 00:02:52.551
(upbeat music)

